      "5.3 A ladder of demand signals\nUse this ladder to interpret interest:\n| Signal | What it may indicate | What it does not prove |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Views or impressions | People encountered the idea | They care about it |\n| Likes or reactions | Some positive response | They have a real problem |\n| Replies and questions | Active attention or curiosity | Purchase intent |\n| Email sign-up | Willingness to hear more | Willingness to pay |\n| Detailed feedback or interview | Stronger engagement with the problem | Product-market fit |\n| Pre-order or deposit | More concrete buying intent | Sustainable demand at scale |\n| Completed purchase | Actual transaction | Retention or long-term satisfaction |\n| Repeat purchase or referral | Continued value for some customers | Universal product success |",
      "The goal is to gather evidence without manipulating people or overstating what the evidence means.",
      "5.4 A practical waitlist workflow\nStep 1: Define the problem",
